# Copy to .env and fill in real values.

# --- Postgres container (read by docker-compose.yml) ---
POSTGRES_USER=rccf
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_DB=rccf
POSTGRES_PORT=5433

# --- Prisma (read by prisma.config.ts) ---
# Must match the values above. Host is localhost from your machine,
# or `postgres` from another container on the same compose network.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://rccf:PASSWORD@localhost:5433/rccf?schema=public"

# --- API server ---
PORT=4000

# Where the website lives. THE one place its address is configured: the CORS
# allow-list and the link in every signup verification email are both derived
# from it. Moving to a real domain is this line and nothing else.
#
# On a real host it is https://, and two other things have to move with it:
# TRUST_PROXY below, and the web package's VITE_API_URL, which must also be
# https or the browser blocks every API call as mixed content. The API warns at
# startup about the first; the site logs the second to the console.
SITE_URL=http://localhost:5173

# Extra origins allowed to call the API, on top of SITE_URL. Comma-separated.
# 4173 is `vite preview`. Leave it empty and only the site itself may call.
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:4173

# Only set true behind a proxy that overwrites X-Forwarded-For. Left true here
# and an attacker just spoofs the header to dodge the rate limit.
#
# But leave it false behind one and the opposite happens: every request arrives
# from the proxy's address, so the whole internet shares a single rate-limit
# bucket and the fifth signup of the day gets a 429. Anything terminating TLS in
# front of this — nginx, Caddy, Cloudflare, a PaaS router — is such a proxy.
TRUST_PROXY=false

# development | test | production. Usually set by whatever runs the process
# rather than here, and several behaviours key off it: signup refuses to start
# without a mailer in production, and the API warns about a live Stripe key
# outside it.
NODE_ENV=development

# --- Scaling knobs ---
# instances x DATABASE_POOL_MAX must stay under Postgres max_connections (100).
DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10
DATABASE_POOL_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
DATABASE_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS=5000
RATE_LIMIT_MAX=5
RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=600
# Public GET caching. CACHE_S_MAXAGE is what a CDN obeys.
CACHE_MAX_AGE=60
CACHE_S_MAXAGE=300

# --- Contact form email (Postmark) ---
# All three together, or leave all three out. Two of the three and the server
# refuses to start, on purpose: a half-configured mailer accepts messages, tells
# everyone they were sent, and delivers nothing.
#
# Unset is a supported state. Messages are still written to contact_messages and
# read in Prisma Studio; only the notification is missing, and the API says so
# at startup.
#
# POSTMARK_TOKEN is the *Server* API token, not the account token.
# CONTACT_FROM_EMAIL must be a verified Sender Signature on that account —
# Postmark rejects anything else — and is not where replies go: those go to
# whoever wrote in.
#POSTMARK_TOKEN=
#CONTACT_FROM_EMAIL=website@rccf.example.org
#CONTACT_TO_EMAIL=officers@rccf.example.org
# Transactional mail belongs on the outbound stream, not a broadcast one.
POSTMARK_MESSAGE_STREAM=outbound

# --- Signup ---
# The verification link is SITE_URL + /join, so there is nothing to set here.
# It points at the *frontend*: the join page reads the token out of the query
# string and posts it back, which is what keeps a mail scanner following the
# link from spending the verification before the student clicks it.
#
# Minutes a link stays good.
SIGNUP_TOKEN_TTL_MINUTES=120

# Minutes a password-reset or email-change link stays good. Shorter than the
# signup one above, and one number for both because they are the same risk: a
# signup link creates an account nobody had, while either of these takes over an
# account somebody already has. Nobody sits on one of these for two hours — they
# ask for it because they are trying to get in right now.
#ACCOUNT_TOKEN_TTL_MINUTES=60

# Signup verification uses the same Postmark account as the contact form above,
# sent from CONTACT_FROM_EMAIL. With Postmark unset, signup still works in
# development — the API prints the link to its own log instead of mailing it —
# but it refuses to start a signup in production, because an unverifiable
# address means an account nobody can finish.

# --- Discord (the username check) ---
# Both together, or leave both out, for the same reason as the Postmark set.
# Unset is a supported state: the handle is stored exactly as typed and nothing
# confirms it, which the API says out loud at startup.
#
# The bot must be a member of DISCORD_GUILD_ID and needs the Server Members
# Intent switched on in the Discord developer portal — the member search this
# uses is gated behind it and returns 403 without it.
#
# The same bot sends the "your free trial has ended" direct message. It can only
# DM someone it shares a server with, which is everybody it is used for here; a
# member whose privacy settings decline messages from server members answers 403
# for ever, and that is their setting rather than a fault to work around.
#DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN=
#DISCORD_GUILD_ID=

# The role that *is* the officer board. Set it and the club appoints officers in
# Discord: whoever carries this role becomes an OFFICER on the site, at signup
# and on the ten-minute sweep, and whoever loses it stops being one. Needs the
# bot and guild above.
#
# LEAVING IT UNSET IS THE SAFE DEFAULT AND THE ONLY SAFETY VALVE. The first
# sweep after you set it stands down every sitting officer who is in the guild
# without the role, and there is no way for the code to tell a board that has
# not been given the role yet from a board that has been dissolved. So: make the
# Discord role match the board you actually want, then set this, then read the
# first sweep's log before trusting it.
#
# ADMIN is never touched in either direction — that stays a person in Prisma
# Studio.
#DISCORD_OFFICER_ROLE_ID=267371948953042945

# --- Discord (the roles the site hands out) ---
# The three above go Discord → site. These three go the other way: the site
# decides who should carry them and writes them into the guild.
#
#   Members       — anyone whose dues_paid_through is still in the future.
#   Project Leads — anyone holding PROJECT_LEAD on at least one project.
#   Team Leads    — anyone holding TEAM_LEAD on at least one project.
#
# A fourth kind is per project rather than per club, and lives on the project
# row instead of here: Project.discord_role_id, set on the project form.
#
# Each is independently optional, and UNSET MEANS NEVER TOUCHED — no read, no
# write, no mention. That is what lets the club switch one on at a time.
#
# The sync only ever acts on a guild member it can match to a user row, by
# snowflake or by handle. Anyone the site does not know is left alone for ever,
# so people in the Discord who never signed up keep whatever they have. For
# somebody it *does* know the site is authoritative: a hand-added Members role
# on an account with no live dues date comes back off within ten minutes.
#
# The bot needs Manage Roles and its own role must sit ABOVE each of these in
# the guild's role list, or Discord answers 403 and the write never lands.
# MegaMan is at position 41; Officers is at 42, which is why the site cannot
# touch the board even by mistake. Do not move the bot above Officers.
#
# The club's ids, for reference:
#   Officers       267371948953042945   (position 42 — read-only, above the bot)
#   Project Leads  1242121555605979206  (position 37)
#   Team Leads     267372064523026433   (position 36)
#   Members        267373066290593794   (position 35)
# DISCORD_MEMBER_ROLE_ID=267373066290593794
# DISCORD_PROJECT_LEAD_ROLE_ID=1242121555605979206
# DISCORD_TEAM_LEAD_ROLE_ID=267372064523026433

# Work out every change and write none of them, naming each in the log. Set
# this the first time the roles above are configured, read one sweep's output,
# check the removals are the ones you meant, and only then turn it off. Unlike
# every other sweep on this server, this one's mistakes land on other people's
# Discord accounts rather than in a table somebody can go and correct.
#DISCORD_ROLE_SYNC_DRY_RUN=true

# Who the bot DMs about a new print or equipment request: Discord user ids,
# comma-separated. Leave it unset and it falls back to every ADMIN/OFFICER
# account carrying a confirmed discordId, which is the right default — it
# follows the board rather than needing an edit each time it changes. Set it
# only to narrow the list to the people who actually run the printers and the
# loan shelf.
#
# Does nothing at all without DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN and DISCORD_GUILD_ID above.
#OFFICER_ALERT_DISCORD_IDS=

# --- Discord (the lab sign) ---
# The channel that says whether the lab is open. The bot keeps ONE message in it
# up to date and renames the channel to match:
#
#   lab-status-🟢  the lab is open
#   lab-status-🔴  the lab is closed
#
# DISCORD IS THE RECORD AND THE SITE FOLLOWS IT. That is the direction, and it
# is the opposite of how this started. What that message says is what the lab
# is: a flip Discord will not take does not happen, and the ten-minute sweep
# reads the message back and corrects the database row against it. A message
# edited by hand wins over the site; the site is not put back over the top of it.
#
# ONE MESSAGE IN THE CHANNEL, AND IT STILL PINGS. Those look like they cannot
# both hold — Discord notifies on a POST and never on an EDIT, so a sign kept up
# to date for ever reaches nobody, and a sign that posts every evening fills the
# channel until people mute it. The way through is POST NEW, THEN DELETE OLD:
#
#   OPENING posts a fresh message (that post is the @Members ping) and deletes
#   the one it replaces. One in, one out.
#   EVERYTHING ELSE edits — closing, the 10pm curfew, a sweep retry. A new
#   message would mark the channel unread for the whole club, and "the lab shut
#   twenty seconds ago" is an interruption nobody can act on.
#
# NOTHING RE-ANNOUNCES. Whether a post goes out is a property of the press, never
# of the database row, so a sweep retrying a failed push edits rather than
# pinging the club a second time for one evening. The cost is the other
# direction: if the post itself fails, the sign is put right by an edit and that
# opening's ping is lost. A missed notification beats being pinged every ten
# minutes until a rename stops being throttled.
#
# The ten-minute sweep also TIDIES: every message of the bot's in this channel
# that is not the current sign is deleted, five per tick. That is what clears a
# backlog left by an older design or by a delete that failed, and it is the only
# destructive thing here — it only ever touches messages Discord says this bot
# posted, and only in this channel.
#
# The ping needs DISCORD_MEMBER_ROLE_ID set, and it needs the Members role to be
# marked "Allow anyone to @mention this role" OR the bot to hold MENTION
# EVERYONE. Without either, the mention still renders — as flat grey text that
# notifies nobody — which is the failure mode worth knowing in advance, because
# it looks exactly like it worked. With the role unset the message still posts,
# so the unread mark on the channel is the signal.
#
# Three permissions, and they are different ones: SEND MESSAGES in this channel
# for the message, READ MESSAGE HISTORY so the bot can find its own messages
# again, MANAGE CHANNELS for the name. Deleting the bot's own messages needs
# nothing beyond seeing the channel. Each refusal is logged saying which.
#
# WORTH KNOWING BEFORE YOU WONDER: Discord allows TWO CHANNEL RENAMES PER TEN
# MINUTES, per channel. Press the button three times in a minute and the third
# comes back 429 with a retry measured in minutes — and because the rename is
# what the flip is gated on, THE LAB IS THEN LEFT EXACTLY AS IT WAS. Whoever
# pressed is told so: the dashboard gets a 429 with the wait in it and a button
# press gets a private note in the channel. The ten-minute sweep is the same
# window the limit is measured over.
#
# Optional like everything else Discord here. Unset, officers still open and
# close the lab and the landing page still says which; nothing is posted. The
# API says which of the two it is at startup.
#DISCORD_LAB_CHANNEL_ID=

# Where the sign STARTS, on a database that has never pushed one.
#
# A seed, and it does NOT outrank the row — it cannot, because opening the lab
# posts a new message and deletes the old one, so the id changes every evening.
# Its one job is the first push against a channel that already has a sign in it:
# point this at that message and it is adopted rather than joined by a second
# one. After that it is never read again and clearing it is safe.
#
# It has to be a message THE BOT ITSELF POSTED — Discord lets a bot edit only
# its own messages, so anyone else's id is a 403 on every attempt for ever.
#DISCORD_LAB_MESSAGE_ID=

# --- Discord (the buttons on the sign) ---
# THERE IS NOTHING TO CONFIGURE HERE. The buttons work as soon as the bot token
# above does.
#
# A bot is told about a button press in exactly two ways and there is no third:
#
#   1. THE GATEWAY — a WebSocket the bot holds open to Discord, which delivers
#      the press as an event. No public address, no key, nothing to set up; the
#      connection is authenticated once by the bot token. This is what runs, and
#      it is src/discordGateway.ts.
#   2. AN HTTP INTERACTIONS ENDPOINT — Discord POSTs the press to a public HTTPS
#      URL registered on the application. Needs the API on a real domain and a
#      signature checked on every delivery. For later.
#
# THEY ARE NEVER BOTH LIVE. An application with an Interactions Endpoint URL has
# every press POSTed there and its gateway told NOTHING — so which one runs is a
# fact about the application, read off Discord at startup rather than configured
# here. The startup log says which.
#
# Only officers can press — checked against DISCORD_OFFICER_ROLE_ID, or against
# the site's own role for a club that appoints officers by hand. Everybody else
# gets a private note only they can see, and so does anybody who presses during
# the rename cooldown or after ten at night.
#
# The gateway holds one connection per process. Run several API instances with
# no endpoint URL and each is told about every press, so the lab gets flipped
# once per instance — that is the point at which you register the URL below and
# set DISCORD_PUBLIC_KEY, which turns the gateway off by itself.

# ONLY for the HTTP road. The application's PUBLIC KEY — developer portal,
# General Information, "Public Key". 64 hex characters; not the client secret
# and not the bot token, both of which are the wrong length and both of which
# get pasted here by mistake.
#
# Setting this does not turn buttons on, and leaving it unset does not turn them
# off. It is only read when the application has an Interactions Endpoint URL.
#DISCORD_PUBLIC_KEY=

# --- Sessions (signing in) ---
# Idle timeout, in days. Every authenticated request rolls it forward, so
# somebody who turns up to build nights is never signed out mid-term while a
# browser left on a lab machine still goes stale.
SESSION_TTL_DAYS=30
SESSION_COOKIE_NAME=rccf_session

# `lax` or `none`, and this is the setting that decides whether signing in works
# at all once this is deployed.
#
# Keep `lax` whenever the site and the API share a registrable domain —
# localhost:5173 and localhost:4000 do, and so would rccf.org and api.rccf.org.
# Ports and subdomains are not what "same site" means.
#
# Use `none` only if they genuinely differ. It requires https (browsers silently
# drop a SameSite=None cookie that is not Secure, so sign-in appears to work and
# nobody stays signed in), and it re-opens the cross-site request forgery that
# `lax` closes by itself — the Origin check on every authenticated write is what
# covers that.
SESSION_COOKIE_SAMESITE=lax
# Only to share one session across subdomains, e.g. `.rccf.org`. Unset is
# host-only, which is the tighter of the two.
#SESSION_COOKIE_DOMAIN=

# --- Dues ---
# In cents, because that is the unit Stripe charges in. Served to the browser
# from GET /api/dues/status, so the page can never print a number the server
# would not charge — change it here and nothing else.
DUES_SEMESTER_CENTS=2500
DUES_YEAR_CENTS=5000

# The free trial everybody gets at the start of a fall or spring term. Summer
# has none because summer is free outright.
TRIAL_DAYS=21

# How long after a trial closes the bot will still send the "it has ended"
# message. This exists for the day the feature is deployed: without a floor, the
# first sweep after a mid-semester release messages every unpaid member about a
# deadline six weeks past.
TRIAL_NOTICE_GRACE_DAYS=3

# --- Equipment ---
# How long before a loan falls due the bot reminds the borrower, in hours.
#
# Thirty-six rather than twenty-four, and the odd number is the point: a due
# date is the *end* of the day it names, so a flat day's notice fires around
# midnight the night before, when nobody can do anything about it. This lands
# at lunchtime the day before instead.
#
# Only loans actually checked out are reminded about, and only ones still to
# come — something already overdue needs an officer, not a robot.
RETURN_REMINDER_LEAD_HOURS=36

# --- Tasks ---
# How long after a task's deadline the bot asks about it, in minutes.
#
# A grace period rather than a lead time, which is the opposite direction from
# the loan reminder above: there is nothing useful to say about a task before
# its deadline, and a task ticked off five minutes late should never have been
# chased at all.
#
# The sweep runs every ten minutes, so what actually lands is 30 to 40 minutes
# past the deadline. Nothing about a task goes wrong in that slack.
#
# Everyone assigned is messaged, once per deadline. Moving the due date re-arms
# it; nothing else does, so reopening a task against a deadline that already
# went past does not chase anybody twice.
TASK_OVERDUE_GRACE_MINUTES=30

# How far back that sweep will look, in days.
#
# The floor TRIAL_NOTICE_GRACE_DAYS exists for, and for the same reason: without
# it, the first sweep after this feature is deployed walks every task the club
# has ever left open and DMs people about deadlines from last semester. Three
# days is "you have just missed this"; older than that wants a lead having a
# word rather than a robot pointing at a calendar.
TASK_OVERDUE_LOOKBACK_DAYS=3

# --- Upload ceilings ---
# The three kinds of file the site stores, in megabytes. Files live in Postgres
# as bytea — at club scale that is simpler and safer than a blob store, and it
# is what makes "delete the file when the print is done" one DELETE — but it
# means every upload is buffered through this process, so these caps are what
# keep one request from being a memory event.
#
# Documents get the middle number because a design review with photographs in it
# is genuinely larger than a downscaled gallery image and genuinely smaller than
# a mesh — and unlike both of those nothing shrinks it on the way, since a PDF
# cannot be re-encoded in the browser the way a photograph is.
#
# Raise MAX_PRINT_FILE_MB and check the reverse proxy's own body limit in the
# same sitting: whichever is smaller is the real cap, and nginx's default (1 MB)
# refuses the upload with a 413 that never reaches this server's log.
#MAX_PRINT_FILE_MB=30
#MAX_IMAGE_FILE_MB=5
#MAX_DOCUMENT_FILE_MB=15

# --- 3D printing ---
# What each member gets for their *own* prints in one term, in grams — FDM and
# resin together, because it is one budget rather than one per machine. Prints
# marked for a project are not counted against it at all: those are uncapped by
# the club's decision, on the honour system and the officer's discretion.
#
# The balance is never stored anywhere. It is this number minus the grams an
# officer recorded on that member's finished personal prints for the term they
# were asked in, so changing it here changes everybody's remaining balance on
# the next page load and nothing has to be migrated.
PERSONAL_PRINT_GRAMS=500

# UCF's academic calendar feed. Terms are read from <base>/<year>/<season>, and
# the server falls back to fixed dates when it cannot be reached — the club's
# dues year cannot depend on somebody else's uptime. Override only to point at a
# mirror or a fixture.
#UCF_CALENDAR_URL=https://calendar.ucf.edu/json

# --- Stripe (dues payments) ---
# Optional, like Postmark and Discord: unset, dues simply cannot be paid on the
# site — the page says so and points at an officer — rather than the API
# refusing to start.
#
# A *secret* key, sk_test_... while testing and sk_live_... in production. The
# publishable key is the web package's VITE_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY and must come
# from the same Stripe account, or every payment fails with a mismatched-intent
# error that names neither key.
#
# The API warns at startup if a live key is in use outside production, because
# test and live keys differ by four characters and a live one on a laptop
# charges a real card.
#STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=

# The webhook signing secret, whsec_... Separately optional, because the two are
# configured at different moments: `stripe listen` prints a fresh one every time
# it starts. Without it /api/stripe/webhook refuses every delivery, which is the
# only safe thing an endpoint that cannot verify a signature can do.
#
# A checkout can be walked end to end without any webhook — the confirm-return
# path asks Stripe directly. What is lost is the member who pays and closes the
# tab before it clears.
#
# Locally:
#   stripe listen --forward-to localhost:4000/api/stripe/webhook
#STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Receipts: nothing here to set, but worth knowing before somebody reports it as
# a bug. Stripe emails a receipt automatically in LIVE mode only, and only with
# "Successful payments" switched on under Settings > Business > Customer emails.
# It never emails one for a test payment. The site does not claim it does — it
# links Stripe's hosted receipt, which every successful charge has in both
# modes. Turn that Dashboard toggle on before going live if the club wants
# members emailed as well.
#
# NOTE: adding any key to this file needs the API restarted. `tsx watch` watches
# src/, not .env, so a new key changes nothing until the process comes back up —
# and the server goes on reporting the feature as unconfigured, which looks
# exactly like a key that does not work.

# --- How this box serves the site (compose-level, not read by the app) ---
# These three are read by docker-compose.yml, not by src/core/env.ts. They exist
# because the club's box has no public address of its own: a Cloudflare Tunnel
# running elsewhere on the LAN reaches it on one port, so nginx serves the built
# frontend and hands /api to the API on that same origin. See server/nginx.conf.

# Where the built frontend lives on the host — the output of `npm run build` in
# web/, copied up. Mounted read-only into the web container.
WEB_ROOT=/srv/rccf/web

# The port the tunnel already points at. It is 4173 because that is what the old
# SvelteKit site published, so nothing upstream has to change. The old
# `website` container must stay stopped or the two fight over it.
WEB_PORT=4173

# Loopback-only, and that is load-bearing rather than tidy. nginx reaches the
# API over the compose network by service name, so nothing needs this published
# at all — and with TRUST_PROXY=true the API believes X-Forwarded-For, so
# anything on the LAN that could reach port 4000 directly could put a fresh fake
# address on every request and never meet a rate limit.
API_PORT_RANGE=127.0.0.1:4000-4004
